From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DrummingBird1 <1979036+DrummingBird1@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 13:16:24 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] GCM: a ring wrap holds the end at the walker instead of
 waiting

A title flushes put once a frame, so when its ring fills, the tail is
most of a frame the walker has not been allowed to read. The buffer-full
callback (cellGcm_fifo_recycle) waited for the walker to read all of it,
which serialised the title with the walker: in Sonic the Hedgehog's story
scene, 1.4-35 ms once every 27 frames (330 ms in 45 s), and a missed
vblank each time. The SDK's callback only waits for the RSX to leave the
room it is about to write. Now the wrap writes the JUMP, holds the
context's end just short of the walker's position in the tail, moves put
and current to begin and returns; the walker gives the end back when it
takes the JUMP (or on a resync). A title that reaches the held end first
waits there, as a wrap used to, and goes on without wrapping again. With
the walker too close to begin, the old wait stays. The callback now gets
the reserve size (r4). GCM_RECYCLE_WAIT=1 restores the old behaviour.
New test: test_gcm_recycle_hold.

Measured on the story scene over 45 s: recycle waits 323-344 ms -> 0,
29.08-29.49 -> 29.58-29.64 flips/s; Wave Ocean 59.23-59.83 -> 59.71-59.85.

The tools that found it:
- PS3_FTRACE gets the D3D12 backend's spans (GPU wait, encode, present),
  why each drain pass stopped and how far it went, and the recycle wait.
- PS3_SAMPLE_RVAS_TIME=1 stamps each sample with the PS3_FTRACE clock,
  the leaf module and the first three callers in the executable, unwound;
  PS3_SAMPLE_DELAY=<s> starts sampling late, so it does not slow the boot.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
